# Cloudflare Pages routing.
#
# SERVE THE MAGAZINE AT THE ROOT, with no redirect at all.
#
# The site used to answer "/" with a stub page whose JavaScript then sent the
# browser on to /unplug-magazine. That hop cost three things:
#
#   1. SEO. Search engines were told the homepage was a redirect to somewhere
#      else, so the address people actually share was never the indexed one.
#   2. The query string. The stub had to be taught to carry "?" through by
#      hand, and a campaign link pointing at the bare domain arrived with its
#      utm tags already stripped until that was fixed.
#   3. The referrer. After the hop, the next page's referrer was OUR OWN
#      domain, so a click from Instagram to unplugnews.com was recorded as
#      Direct — under-reporting every social channel.
#
# A 200 here is a REWRITE, not a redirect: Cloudflare serves the magazine's
# bytes at "/" while the address bar, the query string and the referrer all
# stay exactly as the visitor arrived with them. Nothing to carry through by
# hand, because nothing is being handed anywhere.
#
# index.html is deliberately left in place. If this rule ever fails to apply,
# the old stub still answers and the site still works — a broken homepage is
# not a risk worth taking for a tidier tree.
# The target is the EXTENSIONLESS path. Pages serves /unplug-magazine.html
# as a 308 to /unplug-magazine, and a rewrite whose destination is itself
# a redirect is not one Pages will follow.
/    /unplug-magazine    200

# /nominate — the one page with a real path rather than ?p=.
#
# It is the address that goes in a social bio, and "unplugnews.com/nominate"
# is one somebody can read out loud over a phone. A 200 here is a REWRITE for
# the same reason the root rule is: Pages serves the magazine's bytes at
# /nominate while the address bar keeps saying /nominate, so a refresh, a
# bookmark and a shared link all work. A 301 to ?p=nominate would put the
# query string back in front of everybody who followed the clean link.
#
# The trailing-slash form is listed separately: Pages does not treat
# /nominate and /nominate/ as the same request, and somebody typing the
# address by hand adds the slash about half the time.
/nominate     /unplug-magazine    200
/nominate/    /unplug-magazine    200

# /form/<slug> — an admin-built form at its own address.
#
# A wildcard rather than a list, because the slug is whatever an admin typed
# and Pages cannot be redeployed every time somebody makes a form. Same 200
# rewrite as the rest: the address bar keeps saying /form/bursary-2026, so the
# link can be shared, bookmarked and refreshed.
/form/*    /unplug-magazine    200
